From: openSUSE packaging
Subject: Use only the distribution's Node.js
Patch-mainline: Never, downstream packaging policy
References: https://github.com/zed-industries/zed

Zed downloads its own Node.js into the user's data directory when it cannot
find a suitable one. A packaged build must not fetch and execute a second
runtime behind the user's back, and the distribution already provides one
(the package Requires nodejs and npm).

Upstream marks this value "TODO: Expose this setting"; when it becomes a
setting this patch can be replaced by shipping that default instead.

--- a/crates/zed/src/main.rs
+++ b/crates/zed/src/main.rs
@@ -534,8 +534,10 @@
             let settings = &ProjectSettings::get_global(cx).node;
             let options = NodeBinaryOptions {
                 allow_path_lookup: !settings.ignore_system_version,
-                // TODO: Expose this setting
-                allow_binary_download: true,
+                // Downstream: the distribution packages Node, so never fetch a
+                // second copy at run time. Users can still point node.path at
+                // their own build.
+                allow_binary_download: false,
                 use_paths: settings.path.as_ref().map(|node_path| {
                     let node_path = PathBuf::from(shellexpand::tilde(node_path).as_ref());
